<p><strong>You can know who is working where without calling anyone when attendance is recorded in the system and a transfer notifies the branch that receives the therapist.</strong></p>
<p><em>The scene below is a typical one, not a single named customer.</em></p>
<h2>"Is she in today?"</h2>
<p>The owner asks a manager whether a therapist came in. The manager checks the notebook, then asks the front desk. At the other branch, a transferred therapist turns up and nobody was expecting her. Both problems come from the same gap: the information exists, but not in one place.</p>
<h2>Why it happens</h2>
<ul><li>Attendance is a paper register at each branch.</li><li>Transfers are agreed by phone and not passed on.</li><li>The owner finds out after the fact.</li></ul>
<h2>How it is solved</h2>
<p>With Zennly, the system records when staff <strong>come in, go out or are absent</strong>. When a therapist is transferred from one branch to another, <strong>the receiving branch gets a notification</strong>, so it is ready before she arrives. See <a href="/features/staff-management/">staff scheduling and transfers</a>.</p>
<h2>Temporary and permanent transfers</h2>
<p>A transfer can be temporary or permanent. During a temporary one the therapist is locked at their home branch and opens to bookings again when the time is over; you can extend it or recall her early. Read <a href="/blog/therapist-visiting-another-branch-attendance-nepal/">how a visiting therapist is handled</a>.</p>
<h2>From attendance to pay and reports</h2>
<p>The same records feed <a href="/features/payroll/">payroll</a>, so absences are not rebuilt from memory at month end, and attendance appears in the built-in <a href="/features/reports/">reports</a> across every branch.</p>
<h2>A quick fix you can start today</h2>
<ol><li>Mark in, out and absence every day in one shared record.</li><li>Tell the receiving branch about every transfer before it happens.</li><li>Review attendance weekly, not only at payroll time.</li></ol>
